(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacodynamics-exam-2-pharmacodynamics-master-exam-version-3"]={"title":"Pharmacodynamics Comprehensive Quiz — Set 3","category":"Pharmacodynamics","questions":[{"q":"Which sequence correctly lists the ADME processes of pharmacokinetics?","choices":["Absorption, Diffusion, Metabolism, Equilibrium","Activation, Delivery, Metabolism, Excretion","Adsorption, Diffusion, Modification, Elimination","Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism, Elimination"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ADME = Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism, Elimination — the body's actions on the drug. — PK I & II, Slide 4"},{"q":"Permeation by passive diffusion is fundamentally driven by:","choices":["ATP hydrolysis","The concentration gradient from high to low","A specific membrane carrier","Vesicle formation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Passive diffusion is driven by the concentration gradient, moving drug from high to low concentration. — PK I & II, Slide 7"},{"q":"In Fick's law, a LARGER concentration gradient (C1 − C2) results in:","choices":["Faster diffusion/absorption","Slower diffusion","No change in flux","Reversal of diffusion direction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Flux is proportional to the concentration gradient, so a larger gradient means faster diffusion/absorption. — PK I & II, Slide 10-11"},{"q":"The protonated (uncharged) form of aspirin, a weak acid, is described as:","choices":["More lipid-soluble","More water-soluble","Unable to cross any membrane","Charged at low pH"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In acid, the protonated form of aspirin is uncharged and more lipid-soluble. — PK I & II, Slide 18"},{"q":"The MRP1-MRP5 transporters play an important role in:","choices":["Passive lipid diffusion of steroids","Excretion of drugs/metabolites into urine and bile","Fick's-law diffusion across capillaries","Formation of intrinsic factor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. MRP transporters help excrete drugs and metabolites into urine and bile and take up neurotransmitters at nerve endings. — PK I & II, Slide 22"},{"q":"A drug with a Vd of 5 L (given 50 mg, plasma concentration 10 mg/L) is best characterized as:","choices":["Hydrophilic, remaining largely in central circulation","Lipophilic, distributed widely into tissue","Highly bound to peripheral tissue","Eliminated by zero-order kinetics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vd = 50/10 = 5 L, a small volume indicating the hydrophilic drug stays mainly in central circulation. — PK I & II, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which factor tends to INCREASE the volume of distribution?","choices":["Renal failure with fluid retention","Dehydration","Extensive plasma protein binding","Confinement to the vascular space"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Renal failure with fluid retention increases Vd (as does liver failure altering body fluid). — PK I & II, Slide 32"},{"q":"Metabolic reactions are described as normally being:","choices":["Activating reactions that increase potency","Processes confined entirely to the kidney","Reactions that decrease water solubility","Deactivating, detoxifying reactions yielding less active products"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Metabolism is usually deactivating and detoxifying, producing less pharmacologically active products. — PK I & II, Slide 34"},{"q":"An ACE inhibitor such as enalapril, which becomes enalaprilat, is an example of:","choices":["A zero-order drug","A prodrug","A phase II conjugate","A drug with no first-pass effect"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Enalapril is an inactive prodrug converted by metabolism to active enalaprilat. — PK I & II, Slide 35"},{"q":"Besides the liver, drug metabolism can also occur in the:","choices":["Bone marrow and spleen","Central nervous system exclusively","Lungs, GI tract, skin, and kidney","Adipose tissue only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Although primarily hepatic, metabolism also occurs in the lungs, GI tract, skin, and kidney. — PK I & II, Slide 36"},{"q":"CYP450 isoenzymes are classified into families and subfamilies according to:","choices":["Their substrate specificity","The organ in which they are found","Amino acid sequence homology","Their molecular weight"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. P450 enzymes are grouped into families (>40% homology) and subfamilies (>55% homology) by amino acid sequence. — PK I & II, Slide 45-46"},{"q":"Charcoal-broiled foods are described in the lecture as agents that:","choices":["Inhibit hepatic metabolism","Increase protein binding","Block renal excretion","Induce hepatic metabolism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Charcoal-broiled foods are listed as inducers of metabolism (grapefruit juice is the inhibitor). — PK I & II, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which combination of routes contributes to total body clearance?","choices":["Only renal clearance","Only hepatic clearance","Renal, hepatic, biliary, and other routes summed","Clearance equals filtration minus reabsorption only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. ClTotal = ClRenal + ClLiver + ClBiliary + ClOther. — PK I & II, Slide 58"},{"q":"In first-order elimination, if 1000 units are present and 15% is eliminated in hour 1, the fraction eliminated in hour 2 is:","choices":["Approximately 30%","Still approximately 15%","Approximately 7.5%","0% until the next dose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. First-order elimination removes a constant percentage (~15%) each hour, though the absolute amount declines. — PK I & II, Slide 66"},{"q":"Half-life can be calculated from the elimination constant Ke using:","choices":["t½ = Ke / 0.693","t½ = 0.693 / Ke","t½ = 0.693 × Ke","t½ = Ke × Vd"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. t½ = 0.693/Ke. — PK I & II, Slide 72"},{"q":"A drug falls from 40 mcg/mL at 8 AM to 10 mcg/mL at 4 PM (8 hours). Its half-life is:","choices":["2 hours","6 hours","4 hours","8 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. 40→20→10 is two halvings over 8 hours, so each half-life is 4 hours. — PK I & II, Slide 69"},{"q":"At steady state, clearance functions as the proportionality constant that makes:","choices":["Vd equal to the plasma concentration","The loading dose equal to the maintenance dose","Half-life independent of Vd","Average steady-state plasma level equal to the rate of drug administration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clearance is the constant relating the average steady-state plasma level to the rate of drug administration. — PK I & II, Slide 74"},{"q":"A loading dose is calculated as:","choices":["Cl × dosing interval","Vd × target concentration","Dose / half-life","Ke × Vd"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Loading dose = Vd × target (total) concentration to promptly reach the desired level. — PK I & II, Slide 80"},{"q":"A drug with 90% plasma protein binding and 40 mg in circulation has how much active (unbound) drug?","choices":["36 mg","20 mg","4 mg","0.4 mg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. 40 mg × 10% unbound = 4 mg is the pharmacologically active fraction. — PK I & II, Slide 90"},{"q":"Coagulase-positive staphylococcus, which may be MSSA or MRSA, is:","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Enterococcus faecalis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. S. aureus is the coagulase-positive staph that may be methicillin-sensitive (MSSA) or -resistant (MRSA). — Antimicrobials, Slide 5"},{"q":"A drug that inactivates penicillin-binding proteins to interfere with the final step of cell wall synthesis is a:","choices":["Beta-lactam","Protein synthesis inhibitor","Fluoroquinolone","Sulfonamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beta-lactams inactivate PBPs, blocking the last step of cell wall synthesis. — Antimicrobials, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which is a recognized mechanism of beta-lactam resistance?","choices":["Efflux pump specific to tetracyclines","Ribosomal methylation of the 50S subunit","Beta-lactamase enzymatic cleavage of the beta-lactam ring","Inhibition of DNA gyrase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta-lactamases cleave the beta-lactam ring; other mechanisms include decreased permeability and altered PBPs. — Antimicrobials, Slide 34"},{"q":"Adding a beta-lactamase inhibitor (e.g., clavulanic acid to amoxicillin) primarily:","choices":["Broadens coverage by protecting the beta-lactam from enzymatic degradation","Converts the drug to a bacteriostatic agent","Changes the target from PBPs to ribosomes","Provides gram-negative-only coverage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The inhibitor protects the beta-lactam, adding anaerobe (bacteroides) and MSSA coverage for infections like diabetic foot ulcers and bites. — Antimicrobials, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which class binds the 30S ribosomal subunit?","choices":["Macrolides","Aminoglycosides and tetracyclines","Clindamycin","Chloramphenicol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aminoglycosides and tetracyclines bind the 30S subunit; macrolides, clindamycin, and chloramphenicol bind 50S. — Antimicrobials, Slide 48"},{"q":"Vancomycin's antibacterial spectrum is best described as:","choices":["Gram-negative coverage only","Atypical pathogens only","Broad gram-positive and gram-negative coverage","Gram-positive coverage only"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vancomycin provides gram-positive coverage only, used for MRSA and (oral) C. difficile. — Antimicrobials, Slide 44"},{"q":"Azithromycin is the preferred therapy among macrolides for:","choices":["C. difficile colitis","MRSA bacteremia","Pseudomonas pneumonia","Urethritis due to chlamydia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Azithromycin is preferred for chlamydial urethritis and is more active against respiratory H. influenzae and M. catarrhalis. — Antimicrobials, Slide 54"},{"q":"Clindamycin's coverage notably EXCLUDES:","choices":["Gram-positive aerobes","Gram-positive anaerobes","Gram-negative aerobes","Gram-negative anaerobes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin has NO gram-negative aerobic coverage. — Antimicrobials, Slide 55"},{"q":"Empiric antimicrobial therapy is best described as:","choices":["Therapy given to prevent an infection before it occurs","Therapy started before culture results, based on likely pathogens","Therapy targeting a confirmed organism from sensitivities","Therapy given only to non-infected surgical patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy is begun before definitive culture/sensitivity data, targeting the most likely organisms for the site. — Antimicrobials, Slide 2 (obj f), 22"},{"q":"Which practice is most likely to MINIMIZE the emergence of antimicrobial resistance?","choices":["Routinely double-covering with broad-spectrum agents","Using broad-spectrum prophylaxis liberally","Prolonging therapy well beyond guideline durations","De-escalating and streamlining therapy based on cultures"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. De-escalating/streamlining therapy (guided by cultures) reduces selective pressure and minimizes resistance. — Antimicrobials, Slide 17"},{"q":"The post-antibiotic effect (PAE) is clinically significant because it:","choices":["Allows continued suppression of bacterial growth even after drug levels fall below the MIC","Requires continuous infusion to maintain killing","Only occurs with time-dependent agents lacking PAE","Eliminates the need to consider the MIC"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. PAE allows persistent growth suppression after concentrations drop below the MIC, permitting less frequent dosing (e.g., fluoroquinolones, aminoglycosides). — Antimicrobials, Slide 24, 59"}]};
